+    "english_abstract": "Since most data in the real world can be represented by graphs, research on graph\nrepresentation learning has attracted increasing attention in recent years. By transforming high-dimensional graph data into low-dimensional vectors, graph representation\nlearning can store graph information and access key information of the graph effectively. Utilizing the learned graph representation data can benefit downstream tasks\nsuch as social network, node classification and so on. With the development of deep\nlearning, researchers have proposed a graph representation learning method based on\ngraph neural network, which enables nodes in the graph to simultaneously learn the\nstructural information of the graph and the feature information of neighboring nodes,\nthereby obtaining high-quality and low-dimensional representations. For graph representation learning on graph neural networks, this paper focuses on two issues: graph\nrepresentation learning methods in large-scale sequential graphs and graph neural network optimization training methods for large-scale graphs in distributed scenarios.\nThe scale of graph in the real world is often large and keeps changing, and the\nexisting GNN models are either unable to handle graph representation learning on dynamic sequential graphs, or the training efficiency is low on large-scale graph. To solve\nthe problem of graph representation learning on large-scale dynamic graphs, this paper\nproposes a large-scale sequential graph representation learning method based on graph\nsummarization—GSAERU model. The GSAERU model consists of two parts: ”graph\nrepresentation learning module” and ”time sequence learning module”. First, the dynamic sequence graph is modeled as a sequence of snapshots of the original graph at\neach moment, and then the graph snapshots are input into the graph representation learning module at a single time step. This module compresses the original graph through\n\niv\nthe graph summarization technology, and inputs the compressed new graph into the\ngraph autoencoder, and uses the reconstruction error with the original graph to train\nthe representation of the nodes of the original graph. After the graph representation\non a single time step is obtained, it is input into the timing learning module, and the\nfeature information and dependencies of the original graph in the time dimension are\nlearned through a recurrent neural network, so as to obtain a high-quality representation of large-scale timing graphs. Extensive experiments on four real-world large-scale\ngraph datasets show that the GSAERU model can efficiently generate representations\nof timing graphs. Compared with the traditional graph neural network algorithm, its average memory consumption and average training time are only 9.86% and 13.27%, and\nthe gap with the best performing algorithm in most indicators does not exceed 3.5%.\nUsing distributed technology to accelerate neural network training is also a hot research topic nowadays. The limited memory and computing resources of a single device\noften become a training bottleneck when processing large-scale data. Distributed technology can provide more computing resources to improve training efficiency. However, due to the irregularity of graph data, the subtask division and model training\nmethods in traditional distributed machine learning methods are difficult to be directly\napplied to graph neural networks. Aiming at the optimization problem of distributed\ngraph neural network training in large-scale graph streaming data scenarios, this paper\nproposes a distributed graph neural network optimization training algorithm based on\ngraph summaries——DSGNN model. The DSGNN model adopts the ”leader-worker”\nmode. First, the leader divides the original graph using a graph flow partition algorithm based on edge segmentation and assigns the divided subgraphs to each worker.\nThe workers perform a mini-batch training based on graph summarization technology\nand return the training results to the leader through an attention layer. Finally, the leader\nsummarizes the results and calculates the gradient, and synchronously updates the models on all computing nodes according to the gradient. Based on extensive experiments\non four real-world large-scale graph datasets, the results show that the DSGNN model\ncan greatly accelerate the training of GNN models, and the F1 score on the link prediction task is significantly higher than other distributed graph neural network models.\nkeywords: graph representation learning,deep learning,graph neural network, distributed\ntechnology",
